      "text": "To find the $z$-coordinate of the point on the line that has an $x$-coordinate of 4, we first find the direction vector of the line. Then, we use the direction vector and the point on the line to find the coordinates of the point with the given $x$-coordinate. We then substitute the $x$-, $y$-, and $z$-coordinates into the direction vector to find the $z$-coordinate.\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf{v} &= \\begin{bmatrix} 5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2 \\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix} 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3 \\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\text{Point with } x = 4: \\quad \\mathbf{r}(4) &= \\begin{bmatrix} 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\end{bmatrix} + 2\\begin{bmatrix} 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5 \\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{align*}\n\nTherefore, the point with an $x$-coordinate of 4 has a $z$-coordinate of $\\boxed{-5}$.",
